## 4.9 Control experiment: regrowth of gold nanorods and nanospheres
Regrowth of gold nanorods and nanospheres were performed in deionized water instead of methyl alcohol due to poor solubility. The nanorod solution \((72\,\upmu\mathrm{L},\) extinction \(=\sim41\) ) was diluted with deionized water \(\left(1.4\,\mathrm{\mL}\right)\) and redispersed in \(100\ \mathrm{mM}\) CPC \((200~\upmu\mathrm{L})\) . Aqueous solutions of \(100\ \mathrm{mM}\) CPC \(\left(5\,\mathrm{mL}\right)\) , \(10\;\mathrm{mM}\;\mathrm{HAuCl}_{4}\) \((100\,\upmu\mathrm{L})\) , \(10\;\mathrm{mM}\) ascorbic acid \((150\,\upmu\mathrm{L})\) , and the nanorod solution \((200~\upmu\mathrm{L})\) were sequentially mixed in a \(20~\mathrm{mL}\) scintillation vial. The reaction was monitored via a UV-visible spectrometer, and TEM sampling was conducted. The nanosphere solution \(\left(1.5\;\mathrm{mL},\right.\) extinction \(=\sim1.0\\) ) was concentrated to \(200\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\) . Aqueous solutions of \(100\,\mathrm{\mM}\) CPC \(\left(5\,\mathrm{mL}\right)\) , \(10\;\mathrm{mM}\;\mathrm{HAuCl}_{4}\) \((100\,\upmu\mathrm{L})\) , \(10\;\mathrm{mM}\) ascorbic acid \((150\,\upmu\mathrm{L})\) , and the nanosphere solution \((200~\upmu\mathrm{L})\) were sequentially mixed in a \(20~\mathrm{mL}\) scintillation vial. The reaction was monitored via a UV-visible spectrometer, and TEM sampling was conducted.
## 4.10 Washing treatment of Rod@ZIF and Sphere@ZIF
Rod@ZIF \(\mathrm{[1.2\mL},\) , extinction \(=\sim2.0)\) and Sphere \(@\) ZIF \(\mathrm{[1.2\mL},\) extinction \(=\sim1.1\\) ) were mixed respectively with methyl alcohol \(\left(5\;\mathrm{mL}\right)\) , and centrifuged at \(8{,}000\;\mathrm{rpm}\) for \(10\;\mathrm{min}\) . Supernatant \(\left(5\;\mathrm{mL}\right)\) was removed, and it was redispersed in methyl alcohol. Each solution \((10\,\upmu\mathrm{L})\) was sampled on a TEM grid. This process was repeated up to 6 times.
## 4.11 Characterization
customized Matlab code. An individual particle (gold nanorod, Rod@ZIF, Semi@ZIF, and Sphere@ZIF) was selected and its TEM image was converted to 8 bit. Gaussian blur function with a sigma of 4 pixels was applied to the image. Contrast of such images was maximized through threshold adjustment to clarify the core shape, giving rise to a mask image with white objects and black background. The mask image was processed via our customized Matlab code in order to obtain curvature values of the core particles. The curvature value was defined by osculating circle in the Matlab code. Consequently, curvature distribution and color map were achieved.
